\f3\b\fs36 \cf0 1. The concept shift: yes, and it's forced\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Why it has to exist.
\f1\b0  Some notation first:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Chunk n holds tokens 4n \'85 4n+3.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
u_n is the core's output for chunk n. It depends on every token up to 4n+3.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The core also outputs \uc0\u265 _\{n+1\}, its prediction of chunk n+1.\
\ls6\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The decoder at position t predicts x_\{t+1\}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 If position t could read a core output whose chunk contains x_\{t+1\}, it would read its own answer. So each position must read an 
\f6\i earlier
\f1\i0  concept. That rule is the concept shift, and NCP got it right.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf0 The refinement: align the shift to the target token, not to the input chunk.
\f1\b0  There are two ways to write the shift:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0
\f3\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Chunk-aligned
\f1\b0  (the simple version, which our encoder harness uses): every token in chunk n reads u_\{n\uc0\u8722 1\} 
\f2 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  \uc0\u265 _n.\
\ls7\ilvl0
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Target-aligned
\f1\b0  (what we derived in the concept module): position t reads u_\{j*\} 
\f2 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  \uc0\u265 _\{j*+1\}, where j*(t) = 
\f5 \uc0\u8970 
\f1 (t+1)/4
\f5 \uc0\u8971 
\f1  \uc0\u8722  1.\
