\cf0 At t = 4n+3, the chunk-aligned version hands the decoder a plan about a chunk it has almost entirely read. The plan it actually needs, \uc0\u265 _\{n+1\}, is legal at that point: u_n depends on tokens up to 4n+3 = t, and never on x_\{t+1\}. So the chunk-aligned shift wastes the plan on 1 position in 4. That position is the first token of every new chunk, which is exactly the token the plan was built to predict.\
Geometrically, the target-aligned read is the chunk-aligned one moved 1 token earlier, so the boundary where the plan changes lands where the 
\f6\i targets
\f1\i0  change chunk, not where the 
\f6\i inputs
\f1\i0  do.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

