\f3\b\fs36 \cf0 2. The CRC from encoder to decoder: yes, but only over L-stream taps\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Here the evidence cuts both ways, so it's worth being precise.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf0 The condition.
\f1\b0  A CRC gives each decoder layer a learned mix of encoder layer states. Its reach is the 
\f3\b maximum
\f1\b0  reach over the taps it reads:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
If every tap comes from \{e0, L after S1 \'85 S7\}, every tap has reach \uc0\u8804  63. The CRC then adds no bypass, and the guarantee holds.\
\ls9\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
If any tap is G, or anything after F4 or F8 in G, its reach is the whole past. That's F6 exactly. In Arch 3, switching off the token path plus the enc
\f2 \uc0\u8594 
\f1 dec CRC cost 
\f3\b +4.29
\f1\b0  nats, against 
\f3\b +0.015
\f1\b0  for the plan. The decoder did everything and the core was unread.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 So the rule is: CRC from encoder to decoder is allowed only over e0 and the L states.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf0 Expected value: small.
\f1\b0  Across every CRC we trained, switching it off cost between +0.0005 and +0.008 nats. That's F2: the routers saturate and do nothing. There's also a structural reason. Our decoder 
\f6\i continues
\f1\i0  the L stream, and 
\f4\fs26 tok
\f1\fs24  = zcRMS(L) already is the sum of every S-layer write plus e0. The only thing a CRC adds is the ability to re-weight depths per layer. The one first-principles argument for it is exact token identity. After 7 layers, e0 is diluted inside L, and a direct e0 tap makes copying cheap. That matters for copy-type tasks and names.\

\f3\b My recommendation.
\f1\b0  Include it restricted to the L taps, as a cheap arm. Give it an ablation in the Step 5 tests, and if it measures inert, drop it.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

