\f3\b\fs36 \cf0 The design, step by step\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Step 1: what the decoder has to do.
\f1\b0  It predicts x_\{t+1\} from exactly two things:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls24\ilvl0
\f4\fs26 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
tok
\f1\fs24 : the encoder's token states, which see only the last 63 tokens exactly.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls24\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The plan: everything older, which can only arrive through the concept core.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Nothing else may enter the decoder, because anything else would be a route around the core.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf0 Step 2: when position t may read the core.
\f1\b0  The core's output u_j for chunk j exists only after token 4j+3 has been read, so position t needs 4j+3 \uc0\u8804  t. The newest chunk that satisfies this is\u8232 $$j^*(t)=\\lfloor (t+1)/4\\rfloor-1 .$$\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls25\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
u_\{j*\} predicts chunk j*+1, and that is exactly the chunk that contains x_\{t+1\}.\
\ls25\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
So the plan is always about the chunk being written, and it can never contain the answer x_\{t+1\}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf0 Step 3: what the plan is.
\f1\b0 \uc0\u8232 $$P_t = W_u,u_\{j^
\f6\i \}+W_c,\\hat c_\{j^
\f1\i0 \}+o_\{r(t)\},\\qquad r(t)=(t+1)\\bmod 4$$\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls26\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
u_\{j*\} is the core's state for chunk j*.\
\ls26\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u265 _\{j*\} is the core's prediction of the next chunk.\
\ls26\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
W_u and W_c are learned matrices that map both into the decoder's width.\
\ls26\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
r(t) says which of the chunk's 4 tokens is being predicted, and o_r is a learned vector for each of those 4 slots. One plan has to produce 4 different tokens, so o_r moves it into the right slot for each one.\
\ls26\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
At the first 3 tokens of every document, a learned "null plan" replaces the u and \uc0\u265  terms, so nothing leaks in from the previous document.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf0 Step 4: how tokens are mixed.
\f1\b0  Only sliding-window attention (SWA: each position attends to the last w tokens, here w = 8).\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls27\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Full attention would let the decoder see the whole past directly.\
\ls27\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Linear attention keeps a running memory, S_t = \uc0\u945 _t S_\{t\u8722 1\} + k_t v_t
\f2 \uc0\u7488 
\f1 , in which every past token keeps a nonzero weight.\
\ls27\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Either one would reach past the core, which is the failure you saw in HRM-NCP.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf0 Step 5: each position sees only its own plan.
\f1\b0  The decoder keeps its own state D at each position, starting at D
\f8 \uc0\u8304 
\f1 _t = tok_t + P_t. Each layer does:\uc0\u8232 $$x=\\mathrm\{GRRead\}(D),\\quad q=W_Qx,\\quad k=W_K,\\mathrm\{tok\},\\quad v=W_V,\\mathrm\{tok\},\\quad D\\mathrel\{+\}=s\\cdot\\mathrm\{Attn\}_w(q,k,v)$$\u8232 then a feed-forward layer on D.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls28\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The queries come from D, which holds the plan.\
\ls28\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The keys and values come 
\f3\b only
\f1\b0  from 
\f4\fs26 tok
\f1\fs24 , which holds no plan.\
\ls28\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
So D at one position is never read at any other position. That means:\
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\partightenfactor0
\ls28\ilvl1
\f3\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
A position sees only its own plan.
\f1\b0  In the plain stack, later positions can read up to about 10 earlier plans and combine them, which lets the decoder do the core's composing for it (the failure you saw in IHN).\
\ls28\ilvl1
\f3\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Reach = 63 + (w \uc0\u8722  1) = 70 tokens at any depth.
\f1\b0  In the plain stack, reach grows by 10 tokens per layer.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls28\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
It differs from the NCP-style stack in a single line: where the keys and values come from.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf0 Step 6: the rest.
\f1\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls29\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The same gated residual as the encoder, with one stream.\
\ls29\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Gated attention with QK-norm and partial RoPE, then SwiGLU.\
\ls29\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The plan is added without a gate, so the route can't be switched off.\
\ls29\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Output: logits = zcRMSNorm(D)\'b7E
\f2 \uc0\u7488 
\f1 , reusing the encoder's embedding matrix.\
\ls29\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Loss = NTP + \uc0\u945 \'b7NCP + VQ.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f3\b \cf0 Left out, and why:
\f1\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls30\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Short convolutions: a convolution over D would read the neighbours' plans.\
\ls30\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
IRC, AttnRes and Delta AttnRes: they help deep networks, and at 4 layers there's little for them to fix. The same kind of routers did nothing measurable in the earlier runs.\
\ls30\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The separate plan stream: rejected by the test below.\
\ls30\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The encoder-to-decoder CRC: 
\f4\fs26 tok
\f1\fs24  already contains every encoder layer's contribution.\
\ls30\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
HiLP: our concept core already is its upper level.\
\ls30\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
glu_cross: SwiGLU already multiplies the plan with the token state.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

