\f3\b \cf0 The review (\'a721).
\f1\b0  I re-derived each step and checked every number against the code and run logs, not against the text. The one that mattered most: the draft gave the NCP target as $\\hat c_\{m+1\}$, the core's own prediction, because one symbol stood for two things. The correct target is the encoder's next concept $c_\{m+1\}$; I checked it against the code and fixed the notation throughout. The other seven were smaller: an off-by-one in the near-past range, an approximate logit-gap formula, overstated $\\hat c$-off numbers, a symbol clash with the vocabulary size $V$, a probe credited to the wrong checkpoint, and two claims stated without their limits. \'a721 lists each one with its fix.\
I also checked that the appendix code is byte-identical to 
\f4\fs26 hrm_decoder.py
\f1\fs24  and still passes its tests, and that all four diagrams parse. The review scores the design 8.3/10. What's missing for a higher score is language-model evidence at width 512 on the M2, and a concept module that passes the 4-fact recall gate.\
Four decisions are yours (\'a720): adopt the confined decoder, keep NextLat optional, keep $\\hat c$ in the plan, and the M2 size of 4 layers with window 8.\
The next phase is the concept module's retrieval and capacity gate (\'a720, G-D3), since that's the bottleneck the tests exposed. 
